WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:17.140
Authentication architecture includes login flows, registration flows, password reset flows, identity provider integration, callback endpoints, redirects, cookies, headers, API boundaries, frontend and backend boundaries, and authentication middleware.

2
00:00:17.240 --> 00:00:20.280
These pieces should work together as one clear system.

3
00:00:20.380 --> 00:00:32.780
If every route, worker, or service invents its own identity checks, reviewers cannot easily tell which requests are authenticated, which claims are trusted, and which paths are accidental exceptions.

4
00:00:33.060 --> 00:00:35.380
Trust boundaries are central to authentication.

5
00:00:35.480 --> 00:00:40.760
A browser request crossing into a Python backend is not automatically trustworthy.

6
00:00:40.860 --> 00:00:46.060
A header set by a client should not be treated like a header set by a trusted proxy.

7
00:00:46.160 --> 00:00:50.960
A claim from a validated identity token is different from a form field named role.

8
00:00:51.060 --> 00:00:55.540
A callback from an identity provider is different from an arbitrary redirect request.

9
00:00:55.640 --> 00:01:01.800
Good architecture labels these boundaries and validates identity information at the point where trust changes.

10
00:01:02.080 --> 00:01:09.200
Authentication decisions should be centralized where practical, explicit in code, and hard to bypass accidentally.

11
00:01:09.300 --> 00:01:18.180
Middleware, framework-supported decorators, dependency injection, route guards, and shared identity services can all help when used consistently.

12
00:01:18.280 --> 00:01:27.480
The exact mechanism varies by framework, but the goal is the same: sensitive handlers should not need to guess whether identity was established correctly somewhere else.

13
00:01:27.760 --> 00:01:31.040
Architecture should also account for failure and edge cases.

14
00:01:31.140 --> 00:01:34.420
What happens when the identity provider is unavailable?

15
00:01:34.520 --> 00:01:36.680
What happens when a callback is malformed?

16
00:01:36.780 --> 00:01:39.660
Which endpoints are intentionally public?

17
00:01:39.760 --> 00:01:42.400
How are service-to-service calls authenticated?

18
00:01:42.500 --> 00:01:45.060
Where is authentication state stored?

19
00:01:45.160 --> 00:01:47.400
Clear answers make the system testable.

20
00:01:47.500 --> 00:01:53.500
They also prevent developers from adding one-off checks that work in the happy path but fail under pressure.
